2026-10-18 07:56:57 | task=exec:t1 | iter=14 | call=record_finding({"location": "net/smc/smc_loopback.c:163", "finding": "Use-after-free race: smc_lo_move_data accesses rmb_node after dropping read_lock_bh. At line 163, `conn = smcd->conn[rmb_node->sba_idx]` dereferences rmb_node after `read_unlock_bh(&ldev->dmb_ht_lock)` at line 161. Between releasing the lock and this access, smc_lo_unregister_dmb can acquire the write lock, delete the node from the hash table, kfree(dmb_node->cpu_addr), and kfree(dmb_node). This is a clear use-after-free. Compare with smcd_handle_irq in smc_ism.c:526 which accesses smcd->conn[dmbno] while holding smcd->lock.\n\nThe fix requires either: (1) holding a reference to the node (e.g., RCU or refcount) to extend its lifetime past lock release, or (2) reading sba_idx before releasing the lock and using it safely after.", "dimension": "concurrency and node lifetime management"}) | ok=True
2026-10-18 07:57:01 | task=exec:t1 | iter=15 | call=record_finding({"location": "net/smc/smc_loopback.c:159", "finding": "Missing bounds check on offset+size vs buffer length. The memcpy at line 159:\n```\nmemcpy((char *)rmb_node->cpu_addr + offset, data, size);\n```\ndoes not validate that `offset + size <= rmb_node->len`. If offset is near the end of the buffer and size is large, this writes past the allocated buffer, causing memory corruption. The caller (smc_ism_write -> smcd_tx_ism_write) passes offset from `conn->tx_off + offset` and len from the user, neither of which is bounded against the DMB length in the loopback path. This is a remote memory corruption vulnerability.", "dimension": "bounds checking and write atomicity"}) | ok=True
2026-10-18 07:57:06 | task=exec:t1 | iter=16 | call=record_finding({"location": "net/smc/smc_loopback.c:163", "finding": "Unprotected access to smcd->conn[] array. At line 163, `conn = smcd->conn[rmb_node->sba_idx]` reads from the connection array without holding smcd->lock. The regular ISM implementation in smc_ism.c:100 (smc_ism_set_conn), line 113 (smc_ism_unset_conn), and line 526 (smcd_handle_irq) all access smcd->conn[] while holding smcd->lock. Without this protection, the pointer read could be stale or point to freed memory between the null check and the conn->killed access at line 164. This could lead to a use-after-free crash.", "dimension": "connection notification and tasklet scheduling"}) | ok=True
2026-10-18 07:57:11 | task=exec:t1 | iter=17 | call=record_finding({"location": "net/smc/smc_loopback.c:150-161", "finding": "Excessive lock hold time during memcpy. The read_lock_bh is held from line 150 through line 161 (covering the hash lookup, memcpy, and unlock). The memcpy at line 159 copies user-controlled data and holds the lock during the entire operation. This blocks all writers (register_dmb/unregister_dmb) from acquiring the write lock, potentially starving DMB registration/unregistration. For large DMBs, this could cause significant latency spikes. Consider releasing the lock after copying the token/sba_idx to a local variable, then doing the memcpy outside the lock, or using RCU to eliminate the need for the lock during the hot path.", "dimension": "lock granularity and hot-path performance"}) | ok=True
2026-10-18 07:57:16 | task=exec:t1 | iter=18 | call=record_finding({"location": "net/smc/smc_loopback.c:21", "finding": "SMC_DMA_ADDR_INVALID as ~(dma_addr_t)0 may not be portable. The constant ~(dma_addr_t)0 is used to indicate an invalid DMA address for loopback-ism where no real DMA mapping exists. While this works on architectures where dma_addr_t is 32 or 64 bits, the SMC core and ISM framework may pass dma_addr to DMA mapping/sync APIs. If any code path passes this sentinel to dma_map_single(), dma_sync_single_for_cpu(), or similar, it could cause undefined behavior on architectures where ~(dma_addr_t)0 is a valid DMA address (e.g., on systems with limited DMA addressing where the top address range is valid). The ISM framework should be checked to verify it handles this sentinel correctly.", "dimension": "DMA address semantics and validation"}) | ok=True
2026-10-18 07:57:20 | task=exec:t1 | iter=19 | call=record_finding({"location": "net/smc/smc_loopback.c:275-278", "finding": "Missing cleanup of DMB nodes on module exit. smc_lo_dev_exit() calls smcd_lo_unregister_dev() which only frees smcd->conn and smcd itself. It does not iterate through the dmb_ht hash table to free registered smc_lo_dmb_node structures or their cpu_addr allocations. If any DMBs were registered during the module's lifetime, they will leak. Compare with smc_ism_unregister_dmb which properly frees resources. The exit path should either: (1) iterate and free all hash table entries, or (2) ensure unregister_dmb is called for all registered DMBs before exit.", "dimension": "memory allocation and error unwind paths"}) | ok=True
2026-10-18 07:57:25 | task=exec:t1 | iter=20 | call=record_finding({"location": "net/smc/smc_loopback.c:164", "finding": "Race on conn->killed check without lock protection. At line 164, `if (conn && !conn->killed)` checks the connection's killed flag without holding smcd->lock. The killed flag is set in smc_conn_kill() (net/smc/smc_core.c:1443) and the connection can be freed independently. Even if conn is non-NULL at line 164, it could be freed before line 165 `tasklet_schedule(&conn->rx_tsklet)`. The regular ISM implementation (smcd_handle_irq in smc_ism.c:526-528) holds smcd->lock during the entire conn read and tasklet schedule, preventing this race. The loopback path should similarly hold smcd->lock when accessing conn and scheduling its tasklet.", "dimension": "connection notification and tasklet scheduling"}) | ok=True
2026-10-18 07:57:31 | task=exec:t1 | iter=21 | call=record_finding({"location": "net/smc/smc_loopback.c:163", "finding": "sba_idx used to index smcd->conn without bounds validation at access time. While smc_lo_register_dmb validates sba_idx < SMC_LO_MAX_DMBS (5000) during allocation, smc_lo_move_data uses rmb_node->sba_idx (originally set during registration) to index smcd->conn[] without re-validating. If a DMB node was registered before SMC_LO_MAX_DMBS was changed, or if the token lookup finds a stale/corrupted node, the index could be out of bounds. Additionally, the conn array is sized to max_dmbs (SMC_LO_MAX_DMBS) in smcd_lo_alloc_dev, but there's no runtime check that rmb_node->sba_idx < ARRAY_SIZE(smcd->conn). The regular ISM path relies on the hardware IRQ handler providing a valid dmbno from the device itself, whereas the loopback path trusts a stored index from a user-controlled registration.", "dimension": "index bounds and connection array mapping"}) | ok=True
