2026-10-18 08:02:02 | task=fp-filter | iter=20 | call=record_verdict({"finding": "[DMA address semantics and validation] net/smc/smc_loopback.c:21\n\nSMC_DMA_ADDR_INVALID as ~(dma_addr_t)0 may not be portable. The constant ~(dma_addr_t)0 is used to indicate an invalid DMA address for loopback-ism where no real DMA mapping exists. While this works on architectures where dma_addr_t is 32 or 64 bits, the SMC core and ISM framework may pass dma_addr to DMA mapping/sync APIs. If any code path passes this sentinel to dma_map_single(), dma_sync_single_for_cpu(), or similar, it could cause undefined behavior on architectures where ~(dma_addr_t)0 is a valid DMA address (e.g., on systems with limited DMA addressing where the top address range is valid). The ISM framework should be checked to verify it handles this sentinel correctly.", "impact": "low", "verdict": "drop", "reason": "False positive: SMC_DMA_ADDR_INVALID is only stored in dmb->dma_addr and dmb_desc->dma_addr, never passed to DMA mapping/sync APIs. smc_ism_unregister_dmb only checks if dma_addr is non-zero before calling unregister_dmb, and the loopback unregister_dmb doesn't use dma_addr at all.", "proof": "In smc_ism.c:204: `if (!dmb_desc->dma_addr) return rc;` — only checks non-zero. In smc_ism.c:211: `dmb.dma_addr = dmb_desc->dma_addr;` passes it to ops->unregister_dmb, but smc_lo_unregister_dmb (lines 113-137) never reads dmb.dma_addr. The value is only stored and never used for DMA operations."}) | ok=True
